From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] ui: when the VM takes the pointer without a click, and when the Mac's cursor hides

What: a header with no AppKit in it: when the VM's window takes an absolute
pointer by itself (a motion over the VM, or the window key, the app active
or full screen with the pointer on it; not during a click, not after
Ctrl+Alt+G until the pointer left), and when the Mac's cursor is hidden over
the VM (only once the guest draws its own pointer: its display agent said
hello since the last reset). Used by omacvm-cocoa-pointer-start.patch (which
explains the why).

Why its own file: so the logic is tested against the start, reboot, focus
and let-go sequences without QEMU or a display
(app/runtime/Tests/display/test-pointer-start.sh applies this patch to an
empty folder; at build time and in CI).

Upstream: not applicable (OmacVM's pointer policy).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/display/test-pointer-start.c.

diff --git a/ui/omacvm-pointer-start.h b/ui/omacvm-pointer-start.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8680e0c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ui/omacvm-pointer-start.h
@@ -0,0 +1,100 @@
+/*
+ * OmacVM: when the VM's window takes the pointer by itself, and when the
+ * Mac's cursor is hidden over it.
+ *
+ * No AppKit in here, so it is tested on its own
+ * (app/runtime/Tests/display/test-pointer-start.c); ui/cocoa.m feeds it.
+ *
+ * Upstream QEMU takes an absolute pointer (virtio-tablet) only when the
+ * mouse enters the view, or on a click. After a VM start the window opens
+ * under the pointer; after a guest reboot, or when the window becomes key
+ * again, the pointer is already on it. Nothing enters, so the guest saw no
+ * motion and its pointer sat in the middle until the user clicked once.
+ * Now the first motion over the VM takes it, and so does the window
+ * becoming key (or the app active, or full screen) with the pointer on it.
+ *
+ * The Mac's cursor: when the guest draws its own pointer (show-cursor=off),
+ * it was hidden as soon as the VM had the pointer, also while the guest was
+ * still booting and drew none: no pointer at all. Now it is hidden only
+ * once the guest says it draws its pointer (Omarchy's display agent said
+ * hello on the display port), and shown again from a guest reset until it
+ * says so again. Both pointers sit at the same place meanwhile (absolute).
+ */
+#ifndef OMACVM_POINTER_START_H
+#define OMACVM_POINTER_START_H
+
+#include <stdbool.h>
+
+/* What ui/cocoa.m knows at a motion or at a moment it may take the pointer. */
+typedef struct OmacVMPointerIn {
+    bool absolute;  /* the guest's pointer is absolute (virtio-tablet) */
+    bool grabbed;   /* the VM has the pointer now */
+    bool active;    /* QEMU is the app in front */
+    bool key;       /* one of our windows is key */
+    bool over;      /* the pointer is on the VM's picture, nothing above it */
+    bool buttons;   /* a mouse button is down (a click or drag in progress) */
+} OmacVMPointerIn;
+
+typedef struct OmacVMPointerStart {
+    bool off;       /* OMACVM_POINTER_START=0: upstream's way (enter or click) */
+    bool let_go;    /* the user let go (Ctrl+Alt+G) and has not left the VM */
+    bool drawing;   /* the guest draws its own pointer (said hello since reset) */
+} OmacVMPointerStart;
+
+/*
+ * Take the pointer now? Only an absolute one: taking a relative pointer
+ * captures the Mac's cursor, which stays a click (as upstream). Not during
+ * a click (the click itself takes it, as before) nor after the user let go
+ * with the pointer still on the VM.
+ */
+static inline bool omacvm_pointer_take(const OmacVMPointerStart *s,
+                                       const OmacVMPointerIn *in)
+{
+    return !s->off && in->absolute && !in->grabbed && in->active && in->key &&
+           in->over && !in->buttons && !s->let_go;
+}
+
+/* The user let go of the pointer (Ctrl+Alt+G): not taken again while on the VM. */
+static inline void omacvm_pointer_let_go(OmacVMPointerStart *s)
+{
+    s->let_go = true;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The pointer left the VM, our windows stopped being key (another app,
+ * another Space), or the VM took the pointer: a let-go is over.
+ */
+static inline void omacvm_pointer_left(OmacVMPointerStart *s)
+{
+    s->let_go = false;
+}
+
+/* The guest's display agent said hello: Omarchy draws its pointer now. */
+static inline void omacvm_pointer_guest_hello(OmacVMPointerStart *s)
+{
+    s->drawing = true;
+}
+
+/* The guest was reset: firmware and boot draw no pointer. */
+static inline void omacvm_pointer_guest_reset(OmacVMPointerStart *s)
+{
+    s->drawing = false;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Is the Mac's cursor hidden? hide_setting: the guest draws its own pointer
+ * (show-cursor=off; OmacVM.app sets it for VMs with guest-pointer). A
+ * relative pointer holds the Mac's cursor still, so it is always hidden
+ * then (upstream); an absolute one only once the guest draws its own.
+ */
+static inline bool omacvm_pointer_hide_mac(const OmacVMPointerStart *s,
+                                           bool hide_setting, bool grabbed,
+                                           bool absolute)
+{
+    if (!grabbed || !hide_setting) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    return !absolute || s->off || s->drawing;
+}
+
+#endif
